        "document_id": "P1",
        "block_id": "b0026",
        "quote": "Given these pieces of evidence, by considering the content of tweets, we can see in tweets IDs 3, 4, and 9 that our BERT-based classifier can discriminate tweets in which neither and implicit hatred content exist."
      }
    ],
    "explanation": "The authors point to collection bias from keyword-based sampling and oversampling of particular language and geography, and annotation bias from labeling disrespectful words without considering speaker identity or dialect. They argue that the model’s behavior on specific tweets—including distinguishing cases with no hate or implicit hate—reveals these biases.",
    "answer": "The authors identify keyword-based sampling and oversampling of African American Vernacular English and US tweets as collection biases, and labeling based on disrespectful words without regard to speaker identity or dialect as an annotation bias. They cite the model’s different treatment of specific tweets (IDs 3, 4, and 9) as evidence that it can capture these biases."
